\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Counts are not continuous, and imputing them as if they were leaves a mark on
the imputations that the pooled coefficients do not show. This vignette is
about choosing the model that the data call for, and about seeing when the
choice was wrong. It covers the model families \textbf{countimp} provides, the
formula interface introduced in version~3, and the diagnostics that report a
draw the data do not support.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{The problem, in numbers}
\label{sec:problem}

Self-reported delinquency, 2064 adolescents, four annual waves. The data ship
with the package as \texttt{crim4w}.

<<data>>=
data(crim4w)
waves <- c("ACRIM", "BCRIM", "CCRIM", "DCRIM")
t(sapply(crim4w[waves], function(x) {
  x <- x[!is.na(x)]
  c(mean = mean(x), var = var(x), zeros = mean(x == 0), max = max(x))
}))
@

Three properties occur together here, and each of them rules out a method that
would otherwise be reasonable:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{counts} --- integer, non-negative, so a normal model can
    return $-0.7$ offences;
  \item \textbf{overdispersion} --- variance three to four times the mean,
    where a Poisson model assumes they are equal;
  \item \textbf{an excess of zeros} --- more than half the sample, more than a
    count distribution with this mean would produce.
\end{itemize}

And the values are missing:

<<missing>>=
round(100 * colMeans(is.na(crim4w[waves])), 1)
round(100 * mean(complete.cases(crim4w)), 1)
@

Thirty-nine percent of the sample is complete. Analysing only those cases
throws away three fifths of the data; the question is what to put in their
place.

\section{Why the choice of model matters}
\label{sec:warum}

The obvious objection to any of this is that it makes no difference: the
regression coefficient comes out much the same whatever fills the gaps. On
these data that objection is half right, and the half that is wrong is the
interesting one.

Take a plain question --- how does delinquency in wave~C relate to sex, school
type and prior delinquency --- and answer it three ways.

<<three-ways, cache=FALSE>>=
model <- CCRIM ~ FEMALE + GY + ACRIM
formulas <- list(CCRIM = CCRIM ~ FEMALE + GY + ACRIM + BCRIM + DCRIM)

## 1. complete cases only
cc <- crim4w[complete.cases(crim4w), ]
f0 <- MASS::glm.nb(model, data = cc)
c(GY = coef(f0)[["GY"]], SE = sqrt(diag(vcov(f0)))[["GY"]], n = nrow(cc))
@

<<countimp-run, cache=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=
## 2. a count model that fits: zero-inflated Poisson
imp <- countimp(crim4w, formulas = formulas,
                family = list(CCRIM = zi_poisson()),
                m = 20, maxit = 5, seed = 2026,
                printFlag = FALSE)
pooled <- mice::pool(with(imp, MASS::glm.nb(CCRIM ~ FEMALE + GY + ACRIM)))
z <- summary(pooled)
z[z$term == "GY", c("estimate", "std.error", "p.value")]
pooled$pooled[pooled$pooled$term == "GY", c("fmi", "riv")]
@

Only \texttt{CCRIM} was given a formula here, so the three other waves keep
the default method for their measurement level; \textbf{countimp} says so at
startup. Section~\ref{sec:formulas} shows how to name a model for each of
them.

The point estimates are close. What differs is everything else: the standard
error, because 1251 more cases contribute, and the fraction of missing
information, which says how much of the uncertainty comes from the gaps rather
than from the sample.

The imputations themselves differ more than the coefficients do. This is what
to look at:

<<compare>>=
describe <- function(x) c(mean = mean(x), var = var(x),
                      zeros = mean(x == 0), max = max(x))
filled <- unlist(lapply(seq_len(imp$m), function(i)
  mice::complete(imp, i)$CCRIM[is.na(crim4w$CCRIM)]))
rbind(observed = describe(crim4w$CCRIM[!is.na(crim4w$CCRIM)]),
      imputed  = describe(filled))
@

A method that draws from the observed values --- predictive mean matching, the
usual default --- cannot produce an impossible value, and that is its
attraction. What it cannot do is reproduce the \emph{structure}: on these data
it returns too few zeros, because it has no notion of a process that generates
them. Where the research question concerns the proportion of non-offenders,
that is the whole finding.

\section{Choosing a model family}
\label{sec:families}

\textbf{countimp} provides four basic families and three restrictions of their
support. The choice is not a matter of taste; it follows from what generates
the counts.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{9.2cm}}
\toprule
\texttt{poisson\_count()} & variance equals the mean \\
\texttt{nb()} & variance $\mu + \mu^2/\theta$: overdispersion \\
\midrule
\texttt{zi\_poisson()}, \texttt{zi\_nb()} & two processes: structural zeros
  \emph{plus} a count process \\
\texttt{hurdle\_poisson()}, \texttt{hurdle\_nb()} & two processes: whether at
  all, then how many \\
\midrule
\texttt{zerotrunc\_*()} & counts that cannot be zero \\
\texttt{bounded\_*()} & counts with a known upper limit \\
\texttt{censored\_*()} & counts observed only up to a limit \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The distinction between zero inflation and a hurdle is worth a sentence,
because both fit an excess of zeros and they mean different things. Under zero
inflation a zero can arise two ways --- the respondent is not the sort of
person who offends, or they are but did not this year. Under a hurdle model a
zero means one thing only: the threshold was not crossed. For delinquency the
hurdle reading is the natural one, in two parts that criminologists name
separately: \emph{onset} and \emph{frequency}.

Which family suits these data is an empirical question, and the imputations
answer it. Same predictors throughout, only the family exchanged:

<<families, cache=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=
families <- list(Poisson = poisson_count(), "neg. binomial" = nb(),
                 "hurdle (NB)" = hurdle_nb(),
                 "hurdle (Poisson)" = hurdle_poisson(),
                 "zero-infl. (Poisson)" = zi_poisson())
tab <- t(sapply(families, function(fam) {
  z <- suppressWarnings(countimp(crim4w, formulas = formulas,
         family = list(CCRIM = fam), m = 20, maxit = 5,
         seed = 2026, printFlag = FALSE))
  describe(unlist(lapply(seq_len(z$m), function(i)
    mice::complete(z, i)$CCRIM[is.na(crim4w$CCRIM)])))
}))
rbind(observed = describe(crim4w$CCRIM[!is.na(crim4w$CCRIM)]), tab)
@

Read the \texttt{zeros} column first. The Poisson model misses it by seventeen
points: with no second process to account for them, it can only produce the
zeros its mean implies. The two-part models land within five points of the
observed share. And the negative binomial does something worse than missing a
column --- it produces draws in the thousands where the largest observed
value is 16. Section~\ref{sec:extrapolation} is about why.

\section{The formula interface}
\label{sec:formulas}

Before version~3, the model was specified through a method name and a
predictor matrix:

<<classic, eval=FALSE>>=
vorlage <- countimp(crim4w, maxit = 0, printFlag = FALSE)
pred <- vorlage$predictorMatrix
pred[, c("id", "RE", "HA")] <- 0
countimp(crim4w, method = c(CCRIM = "zip"), predictorMatrix = pred, m = 20)
@

The name encoded the distribution, the number of levels and the random-effects
structure at once (\texttt{2l.nb.noint.boot} names four decisions), and the
predictors lived in a matrix that had to be edited by hand. Version~3 writes
the same model as one expression per model part:

<<formula-example, eval=FALSE>>=
countimp(crim4w,
         formulas = list(CCRIM = CCRIM ~ FEMALE + GY + ACRIM),
         family   = list(CCRIM = zi_poisson()),
         m = 20)
@

The multilevel structure and the intercept handling come from the formula ---
\texttt{(1 | id)} present or not --- so they leave the name. Seventy of the
eighty-five method names are reachable this way. The old interface is
untouched and remains valid; the names are the backward compatibility.

Two model parts get two arguments rather than a \texttt{pscl}-style pipe,
because \texttt{|} already means grouping inside a random-effects term:

<<two-parts, eval=FALSE>>=
countimp(d, formulas = list(y ~ x1 + x2 + (1 | id)),
            zero     = list(y ~ x1 + (1 | id)),
            family   = hurdle_nb(), draw = "bayes")
@

A restriction of the support belongs to the family, next to the variable it
describes, and is mandatory there:

<<scale, eval=FALSE>>=
countimp(d, formulas = list(visits ~ x1, days ~ x1),
            family = list(visits = censored_poisson(censor = 10),
                          days   = bounded_poisson(bounds = c(0, 5))),
            m = 5)
@

\section{When the model extrapolates}
\label{sec:extrapolation}

The negative binomial produced draws in the hundreds. The natural suspicion is
that $\theta$, the dispersion, is poorly determined --- and it is worth
checking rather than assuming:

<<theta>>=
f <- MASS::glm.nb(CCRIM ~ FEMALE + GY + ACRIM + BCRIM + DCRIM, data = cc)
c(theta = f$theta, se_log_theta = f$SE.theta / f$theta)
@

$\theta$ is determined perfectly well. The cause is not the family but the
\textbf{link}. In a log-link model the predictors act multiplicatively,
\[
  \log \mu = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \dots
  \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
  \mu = e^{\beta_0} \cdot e^{\beta_1 x_1} \cdots,
\]
and here three of the predictors are themselves counts running from zero to
fifteen:

<<extrapolation>>=
b <- coef(f)
count_preds <- c("ACRIM", "BCRIM", "DCRIM")
contribution <- sapply(count_preds, function(v) b[[v]] * max(cc[[v]]))
eta <- b[["(Intercept)"]] + sum(contribution)
rbind(range = sapply(cc[count_preds], max), coefficient = b[count_preds],
      contribution = contribution)
c(eta = eta, mu = exp(eta), largest_observed = max(cc$CCRIM))
@

At the observed maxima of its own predictors the model expects 1380 offences
where the largest value ever recorded is 13. Drawing $\beta$ from its posterior
--- which is what makes the imputation proper rather than a prediction --- widens
this further, and the negative binomial then adds $\mathrm{Var} = \mu +
\mu^2/\theta$ on top. The imputed large values re-enter as predictors in the
next iteration.

This is not a defect of the negative binomial. It is what a richer model does
with the freedom it has: it explains the many zeros through a heavy tail
rather than through a separate process. In estimation that barely shows. In
imputation the tail gets drawn.

Two remedies, and on these data both are needed:

<<remedies, cache=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=
d_log <- crim4w
d_log[count_preds] <- lapply(d_log[count_preds], log1p)
run <- function(data, fam) suppressWarnings(countimp(data,
  formulas = formulas, family = list(CCRIM = fam), m = 20, maxit = 5,
  seed = 2026, printFlag = FALSE))
imps <- function(z, data) describe(unlist(lapply(
  seq_len(z$m), function(i) mice::complete(z, i)$CCRIM[is.na(data$CCRIM)])))
zip <- zi_poisson()
rbind(observed        = describe(crim4w$CCRIM[!is.na(crim4w$CCRIM)]),
      "NB, raw"       = imps(run(crim4w, nb()),  crim4w),
      "NB, log(1+x)"  = imps(run(d_log,  nb()),  d_log),
      "ZIP, raw"      = imps(run(crim4w, zip),   crim4w),
      "ZIP, log(1+x)" = imps(run(d_log,  zip),   d_log))
@

Transforming the count predictors removes the exponential extrapolation;
choosing the family the data call for does the rest. The general lesson: a
model that can do more is not thereby the better one.

\section{Checking the imputations}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

The case above should not depend on someone thinking to look.
\texttt{countimp\_diagnostics()} records every draw and reports those the data
do not support:

<<diagnostics, cache=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=
## Every call to countimp_diagnostics() prints its table; captured here.
quietly <- function(e) invisible(capture.output(e))
quietly(countimp_diagnostics(enable = TRUE))
quietly(countimp_diagnostics(reset = TRUE))
invisible(run(crim4w, nb()))
quietly(rec <- countimp_diagnostics())

c(draws = nrow(rec), flagged = sum(rec$status == "flagged"),
  largest_draw = max(rec$draw_max), observed_max = max(rec$obs_max),
  ratio = round(max(rec$draw_ratio, na.rm = TRUE), 1))
@

The report itself names the variable, the draw and the ratio:

<<diagnostics-excerpt>>=
## as.data.frame() drops the diagnostics class, whose print method would
## summarise the excerpt as if it were the whole record.
as.data.frame(head(rec[rec$status == "flagged",
  c("method", "problems", "obs_max", "draw_max", "draw_ratio")], 3))
@

The flag is \texttt{draw\_far\_above\_observed}, raised when a draw exceeds
the largest observed value more than tenfold. It is \emph{raised, not acted
on}: large values are the tail of the distribution, and discarding them until
a small one arrives would pull the imputations downwards. The threshold is calibrated ---
with a family that fits, the ratio stays below~4 across families and data
situations.

<<diagnostics-off, include=FALSE>>=
countimp_diagnostics(enable = FALSE)
@

Three mistakes are common enough to name:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{The row number as a predictor.} The default takes every
    column, \texttt{id} included. It carries no information and costs a
    parameter.
  \item \textbf{Fully coded categories.} Three school types as three dummies
    sum to one and are collinear with the intercept. \texttt{glm()} sets one
    coefficient to \texttt{NA}; an imputation model cannot.
  \item \textbf{Imputing every wave from every other.} Statistically the
    cleaner choice, but with count models across five iterations it can
    amplify.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Two levels}
\label{sec:two-levels}

Clustered counts --- pupils in classes, patients in wards --- take the same
families with a random-effects term in the formula:

<<two-levels, eval=FALSE>>=
countimp(d, formulas = list(y ~ x1 + (1 | class)),
            family = list(y = nb()), m = 20)
@

A variable that is constant within its cluster (class size, ward type) is a
different matter: it carries one value per cluster but occupies one cell per
row, and imputing it cell by cell destroys that constancy. Those variables
take the \texttt{2lonly} methods through the classic interface:

<<cluster-level, eval=FALSE>>=
countimp(d, method = c(y = "2l.poisson", classsize = "2lonly.pmm"),
            predictorMatrix = pred, m = 20)
@

\section{Beyond R}
\label{sec:kern}

The computational core is available as a standalone C++ program, which makes
countimp usable from software that is not R. It gives bit-identical
results across macOS, Linux and Windows for the same seed --- not something to
take for granted, since \texttt{std::poisson\_distribution} returns different
values per standard library from the same random numbers. The core therefore
computes its distributions itself.

Through the companion package \textbf{rcountimp} the same core writes what
Mplus expects for \texttt{TYPE = IMPUTATION}: one data file per imputation, the
list file naming them, and an input skeleton with the variable names and the
\texttt{COUNT} declaration. Only the analysis model remains to be filled in.

